<h3 class=3D"post-title entry-title" itemprop=3D"name">
From Bollinger to Fractal Bands
</h3>
<div class=3D"post-header">
<div class=3D"post-header-line-1"></div>
</div>
<div class=3D"post-body entry-content" id=3D"post-body-5300964443105994754"=
 itemprop=3D"description articleBody">
Bollinger Bands indicator is a well-known and interesting indicator, as it =
provides with entry and exit points. It basically consists in a MA and two =
bands above and below it. Each band is classically placed at 2 standard dev=
iations away from the MA. If we assume that price variations follow a norma=
l distribution, this ensures that 95% of the prices will fall within the ba=
nds.<br>___________________________________________________________________=
___________________________________<br><br><b>I-Some theoretical points</b>=
<br><br>Keeping this assumption for now, the time-series of price variation=
s can be described by a Wiener Brownian Motion of normal distribution N(0,t=
). It is interesting to see the probability of the prices to be within the =
bands is equal to the probability of the maximum of the price (that we will=
 name M(t)) to be within them, as shown below:<br><br><a href=3D"http://www=
.codecogs.com/eqnedit.php?latex=3DP%28M%28t%29%5Cleq%20x%29=3D1-P%28M%28t%2=
9%5Cgeq%20x%29=3D1-2%281-%5CPhi%20%28%5Cfrac%7Bx%7D%7B%5Csqrt%7Bt%7D%7D%29%=
29" target=3D"_blank"><img src=3D"http://latex.codecogs.com/gif.latex?P%28M=
%28t%29%5Cleq%20x%29=3D1-P%28M%28t%29%5Cgeq%20x%29=3D1-2%281-%5CPhi%20%28%5=
Cfrac%7Bx%7D%7B%5Csqrt%7Bt%7D%7D%29%29" title=3D"P(M(t)\leq x)=3D1-P(M(t)\g=
eq x)=3D1-2(1-\Phi (\frac{x}{\sqrt{t}}))"></a><br><br>For more details and =
the justification of this formula, see <a href=3D"http://stochasticfractals=
.wordpress.com/2009/05/04/maximum-of-wiener-brownian-motion/">my other blog=
</a>.<br>We then see:<br><br><a href=3D"http://www.codecogs.com/eqnedit.php=
?latex=3DP%28M%28t%29%5Cleq%202%5Csqrt%7Bt%7D%29=3D1-2%281-%5CPhi%20%28%5Cf=
rac%7B2%5Csqrt%7Bt%7D%7D%7B%5Csqrt%7Bt%7D%7D%29%29=3D2%5CPhi%20%282%29-1=3D=
erf%28%5Csqrt%7B2%7D%29=3D0.954" target=3D"_blank"><img src=3D"http://latex=
.codecogs.com/gif.latex?P%28M%28t%29%5Cleq%202%5Csqrt%7Bt%7D%29=3D1-2%281-%=
5CPhi%20%28%5Cfrac%7B2%5Csqrt%7Bt%7D%7D%7B%5Csqrt%7Bt%7D%7D%29%29=3D2%5CPhi=
%20%282%29-1=3Derf%28%5Csqrt%7B2%7D%29=3D0.954" title=3D"P(M(t)\leq 2t)=3D1=
-2(1-\Phi (\frac{2\sqrt{t}}{\sqrt{t}}))=3D2\Phi (2)-1=3Derf(\sqrt{2})=3D0.9=
54"></a><br><br>Such probabilities are calculated for the theoretical value=
 of the standard deviation of the WBM, the Bollinger Bands, however, calcul=
ates an empirical value for it using the well known formula:<br><br><a href=
=3D"http://www.codecogs.com/eqnedit.php?latex=3D%5Csigma%20=3D%5Csqrt%7B%5C=
frac%7B1%7D%7BN%7D%5Csum_%7Bi=3D1%7D%5E%7BN%7D%28x_%7Bi%7D-%5Cbar%7Bx%7D%29=
%5E%7B2%7D%7D" target=3D"_blank"><img src=3D"http://latex.codecogs.com/gif.=
latex?%5Csigma%20=3D%5Csqrt%7B%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7BN%7D%5Csum_%7Bi=3D1%7D%5E%7B=
N%7D%28x_%7Bi%7D-%5Cbar%7Bx%7D%29%5E%7B2%7D%7D" title=3D"\sigma =3D\sqrt{\f=
rac{1}{N}\sum_{i=3D1}^{N}(x_{i}-\bar{x})^{2}}"></a><br><br>Given this pract=
ical =CF=83 and the theoretical one, we can equate the two:<br><br><a href=
=3D"http://www.codecogs.com/eqnedit.php?latex=3D%5Csqrt%7Bt%7D=3D%5Csqrt%7B=
%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7BN%7D%5Csum_%7Bi=3D1%7D%5E%7BN%7D%28x_%7Bi%7D-%5Cbar%7Bx%7D=
%29%5E%7B2%7D%7D" target=3D"_blank"><img src=3D"http://latex.codecogs.com/g=
if.latex?%5Csqrt%7Bt%7D=3D%5Csqrt%7B%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7BN%7D%5Csum_%7Bi=3D1%7D=
%5E%7BN%7D%28x_%7Bi%7D-%5Cbar%7Bx%7D%29%5E%7B2%7D%7D" title=3D"\sqrt{t}=3D\=
sqrt{\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=3D1}^{N}(x_{i}-\bar{x})^{2}}"></a><br><br>And knowi=
ng the theoretical standard deviation for a FBM (see <a href=3D"http://stoc=
hasticfractals.wordpress.com/2009/05/05/standard-deviation-of-fractional-br=
ownian-motion/">there</a>), we get the practical standard deviation for FBM=
 (of Hurst parameter H):<br><br><a href=3D"http://www.codecogs.com/eqnedit.=
php?latex=3D%5Csigma%20_%7BFBM%7D=3D%7Ct%7C%5E%7BH%7D=3D%28%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7=
BN%7D%5Csum_%7Bi=3D1%7D%5E%7BN%7D%28x_%7Bi%7D-%5Cbar%7Bx%7D%29%5E%7B2%7D%29=
%5E%7BH%7D=3D%5Csigma%20_%7BWBM%7D%5E%7B2H%7D%5C;%20%5C;%20%5C;%20%5C;%20%5=
C;%20%5C;%20%5C;%20%281%29" target=3D"_blank"><img src=3D"http://latex.code=
cogs.com/gif.latex?%5Csigma%20_%7BFBM%7D=3D%7Ct%7C%5E%7BH%7D=3D%28%5Cfrac%7=
B1%7D%7BN%7D%5Csum_%7Bi=3D1%7D%5E%7BN%7D%28x_%7Bi%7D-%5Cbar%7Bx%7D%29%5E%7B=
2%7D%29%5E%7BH%7D=3D%5Csigma%20_%7BWBM%7D%5E%7B2H%7D%5C;%20%5C;%20%5C;%20%5=
C;%20%5C;%20%5C;%20%5C;%20%281%29" title=3D"\sigma _{FBM}=3D|t|^{H}=3D(\fra=
c{1}{N}\sum_{i=3D1}^{N}(x_{i}-\bar{x})^{2})^{H}=3D\sigma _{WBM}^{2H}\; \; \=
; \; \; \; \; (1)"></a><br><br><br><b>II-Implementation of Fractal Bands</b=
><br><br>A straightforward way to implement Fractal Bands seems to just tak=
e classical Bollinger Bands and merely increase the width of the bands by r=
aising the standard deviation to the power of 2H. However, if we do that, h=
ere is what we get (the MA is the FRASMAv2, the reference period is 30, the=
 blue bands are Bollinger Bands for the same speed) :<br><br><a href=3D"htt=
ps://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEhUTp4hO3Dk9YVX8X_VC=
NBRzfKxqveIpw252XN8hUAyoQ5hrpo3cfOwKaCgMH9yLO6nYjH2zG5hqlKPJZaPeywYsVQkG3Dy=
YHtfOByE0EBVU5gk-r9m480lGy2Am3MDH-tP6_GW8cvNhsE/s1600-h/chart_fractionalban=
ds.gif"><img alt=3D"" border=3D"0" id=3D"BLOGGER_PHOTO_ID_53323629570914939=
86" src=3D"https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEhUTp4=
hO3Dk9YVX8X_VCNBRzfKxqveIpw252XN8hUAyoQ5hrpo3cfOwKaCgMH9yLO6nYjH2zG5hqlKPJZ=
aPeywYsVQkG3DyYHtfOByE0EBVU5gk-r9m480lGy2Am3MDH-tP6_GW8cvNhsE/s400/chart_fr=
actionalbands.gif" style=3D"margin: 0px auto 10px; display: block; text-ali=
gn: center; cursor: pointer; width: 400px; height: 227px;"></a><br><br>I do=
n't find this indicator very useful (not useful at all actually, for me). I=
t seems necessary here to get some perspective about how we wish to improve=
 on the Bollinger Bands. From my point of view, as a day trader, I feel Bol=
linger Bands too narrow, the prices hit them too often, especially in a tre=
nding market, where I would like to get a clear signal only when the trend =
is over. But, with Bollinger Bands, most of the trend occurs outside the ba=
nds, prompting me to close the trade much too early and basically inciting =
me not to ride the trend.<br>Applying equation (1) however, we get the coun=
ter-productive effect of narrowing the bands when in a trend, because, in o=
ur case of price variation, the standard variation is much lower than 1(thi=
s may not be the case for stock exchange, but it clearly is for FOREX), rai=
sing it to a higher power therefore decreases its value proportionally.<br>=
A way out of this quandary is simply to apply the following treatment to th=
e standard deviation from the Bollinger Bands instead of the one from (1):<=
br><br><a href=3D"http://www.codecogs.com/eqnedit.php?latex=3D%5Csigma%20_%=
7Bfinal%7D=3D%5Csigma%20_%7BWBM%7D*%5Calpha%20%5E%7BH%7D%5C;%5C;%20%5C;%20%=
5C;%20%5C;%20%5C;%20%282%29" target=3D"_blank"><img src=3D"http://latex.cod=
ecogs.com/gif.latex?%5Csigma%20_%7Bfinal%7D=3D%5Csigma%20_%7BWBM%7D*%5Calph=
a%20%5E%7BH%7D%5C;%5C;%20%5C;%20%5C;%20%5C;%20%5C;%20%282%29" title=3D"\sig=
ma _{final}=3D\sigma _{WBM}*\alpha ^{H}\;\; \; \; \; \; (2)"></a><br><br>By=
 taking =CE=B1 greater than 1, the higher our H, the wider the bands will b=
e, here is what it leads to (with the same setup as before, and =CE=B1=3D2)=
:<br><br><a href=3D"https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AV=
vXsEip26xAyFg8Gle7qk1cV2MqPoDq6r80DA009Uba3-HgikMSO-t7KtDD9uu078qejr_BF6AeG=
kMOdQkEmSxGrL_RubKG7cEuIRBm0vpLFTmfEBfkSlqJBfYaQNsjQGjc08brjF3eTNxD6q8/s160=
0-h/chart_fractalbands.gif"><img alt=3D"" border=3D"0" id=3D"BLOGGER_PHOTO_=
ID_5332374544491647234" src=3D"https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/=
R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEip26xAyFg8Gle7qk1cV2MqPoDq6r80DA009Uba3-HgikMSO-t7KtDD9uu078=
qejr_BF6AeGkMOdQkEmSxGrL_RubKG7cEuIRBm0vpLFTmfEBfkSlqJBfYaQNsjQGjc08brjF3eT=
NxD6q8/s400/chart_fractalbands.gif" style=3D"margin: 0px auto 10px; display=
: block; text-align: center; cursor: pointer; width: 400px; height: 227px;"=
></a><br><br>The script fractal_bands.mq4 can be downloaded from <a href=3D=
"http://codebase.mql4.com/5590">this address</a> from the MQL4 site.<br>The=
 input parameters of the indicator are as follows:<br><b>e_period (integer)=
</b>: This is the period considered for calculating the fractal dimension, =
default is 30.<br><b>normal_speed (integer)</b>: This is the speed of the S=
MA before being modified to become the FRASMA, default is 30.<br><b>alpha (=
real)</b>: This is the alpha from equation (2), default is 2.<br><b>shift (=
integer)</b>: This is the number of bars the FRASMA is shifted to the right=
(positive) or to the left(negative), default is 0.<br><b>e_type_data (0,1,2=
 or 3)</b>: This is the type of price the indicator will consider (0=3DCLOS=
E, 1=3DOPEN, 2=3DHIGH, 3=3DLOW), default is 0.<br>  <br><br><b>III-Strategi=
cal considerations</b><br><br>I have started using the Fractal Bands indica=
tor, and am very happy of it so far. The strategy is quite straightforward.=
<br>I enter in a BUY position after the price have rebounded (after touchin=
g it) from the lower band and crossed the FRASMA, my Stop Loss is then set =
to the level the prices hit the lower band, and my Take Profit is when the =
prices hit the higher band.<br>Symmetrically, I enter a SELL position after=
 the price have fallen from the higher band (after touching it) and crossed=
 the FRASMA, Stop Loss set at the level of the hit of the higher band, and =
Take Profit when the lower band is hit.<br>It is obviously possible (and ev=
en advised) to make your Stop Loss trailing the price changes.<br>I used th=
is strategy for EUR/USD on a 5 minutes timeframe, using it on other timefra=
mes or on other instruments may require a different setup, mine was to set =
the speed of the FRASMA at 30, and =CE=B1=3D2 (in equation (2) above), it i=
s possible to change these values.
